-- title:
--   Determinant-norm slabs in GL₂(A_F) are Borel
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a number field, with ring of integers $\mathcal{O}_F$ and adele ring $\mathbb{A}_F =$ `AdeleRing (𝓞 F) F`, and let $d_1, d_2$ be real numbers. Write `AdelicGL2 (𝓞 F) F` for $GL_2(\mathbb{A}_F)$, the group of units of the ring of $2\times 2$ matrices over $\mathbb{A}_F$, and equip it with the $\sigma$-algebra [`NumberField.AdelicHaar.glBorel (Fin 2) (𝓞 F) F`](def/NumberField_AdelicHaar.html#L176), namely the Borel $\sigma$-algebra generated by its topology; this $\sigma$-algebra is passed explicitly to `MeasurableSet` rather than inferred. For a unit $x$ of $\mathbb{A}_F$, `ideleNorm F x` is the real number obtained from the value at $x$ of the distributive Haar character of the additive group $\mathbb{A}_F$, i.e. the nonnegative real factor by which multiplication by $x$ scales additive Haar measure. The assertion is that the set of those $g \in GL_2(\mathbb{A}_F)$ whose determinant $\det g \in \mathbb{A}_F^{\times}$ satisfies $d_1 \le \mathrm{ideleNorm}_F(\det g) \le d_2$ is a Borel set. No inequality between $d_1$ and $d_2$ is assumed; for $d_2 < d_1$ the set is empty.
--
--   These slabs are the determinant windows used in reduction theory and in the spectral analysis of automorphic forms on $GL_2$ over a number field; measurability is the basic prerequisite for integrating over them. The result is cited throughout the treatment of cuspidal and pseudo-Eisenstein estimates, where integrals over regions cut out by bounds on the idele norm of the determinant are formed.
-- source:
